    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working with a client who's been stuck at the same weight on their back squat for like 4 weeks now. they're hitting everything else fine but the squat just isn't moving. how do deload weeks or changing up the volume help get past a plateau like this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A plateau, especially when it's stubborn like a squat stall, is a classic sign that your body is either over-fatigued or has become too accustomed to the current stimulus. A proper deload week, which involves significantly reducing volume and intensity, can allow your central nervous system and muscles to recover, making them stronger for the next push. Alternatively, changing up the volume—perhaps by switching from heavy sets to higher reps with lighter weight, or vice versa—can shock the system and force adaptation. Think of it like a small, strategic disruption to the routine that keeps the progress stagnant.",
      "source": "target",
