    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can you explain a bit more about the physiological side of that \"sentient force\" feeling? like, what is actually happening in the body or the nervous system when a plateau feels so aggressive?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"sentient force\" feeling is often the body's sophisticated way of telling you it's reaching its current capacity, which is a very real physiological state. When you hit a plateau, it often means your neuromuscular system is experiencing a kind of localized overload, similar to a small, persistent drain on your energy reserves. Your central nervous system (CNS) gets fatigued from constantly recruiting high levels of motor units to move heavy loads, and this CNS fatigue can manifest as a feeling of profound resistance, making the weight feel incredibly heavy even if it's objectively the same weight.",
      "source": "target",
